  "name": "Clean and Jerk",
  "definition": "A bilateral floor-start barbell Power lift that combines one full squat clean and one split jerk under one unchanged load and one repetition history. The athlete completes the clean to a full front-squat receive, stands with the feet on one line and the knees extended, becomes motionless in the front rack, then performs a split jerk to straight-arm overhead lockout and recovers both feet to one line before the floor reset. The IWF standard directly establishes Clean and Jerk as one lift and owns the clean-to-jerk transition and completion criteria. Exact clean and isolated split-jerk studies support the narrowed phase geometry; no reviewed experiment measures this exact combined repetition end to end. Categorical actions and muscle roles are transparent mechanics and anatomy inferences, not measured contribution rankings. Power-clean catches, hang starts, symmetric jerk receives, clean and press, thrusters, component-only lifts, and complexes with independently logged loads remain outside this family. The 45-pound, 20-kilogram, and three-repetition seeds are product defaults rather than source prescriptions.",
